# Independently authored KILL / BACKUP / RESTORE / PURGE
# sentinel smoke for the GPL MTR-format runner. NOT
# copied or adapted from MySQL's mysql-test/.
#
# Existing coverage (do NOT duplicate):
#   - KILL — the three spellings below, all of them
#     aimed at an id that is not running. What a real
#     KILL kills is not this file's question.
#   - BACKUP DATABASE / RESTORE FROM — NONE.
#   - PURGE BINARY LOGS — NONE.
#   - mysql_maintenance_cmds_sentinel_basic_extra (PR
#     #371) covers FLUSH / RESET / HANDLER / SHUTDOWN.
#
# This slice pins:
#   - KILL <id> / KILL QUERY <id> / KILL CONNECTION <id>
#     → 1094 "Unknown thread id: <id>", which is what
#     MySQL answers for a thread that is not running.
#     KILL is a statement now (#4959); this file was
#     written when all three spellings parse-rejected at
#     1064, so the three lines below are a re-derivation,
#     not a regression. The ids are chosen to not exist.
#   - BACKUP DATABASE / RESTORE FROM / PURGE BINARY LOGS
#     → 1064 with the "Expected: an SQL statement, found:
#     <X>" shape (sqlparser doesn't recognize the leading
#     keyword).
#
# Big-picture finding:
#
#   Four of the seven statements share one sqlparser error
#   template, documented in PR #371 as shape (d)
#   ("Expected: an SQL statement, found: X"): sqlparser-rs
#   does not recognize BACKUP, RESTORE or PURGE as a
#   top-level keyword, so the error fires before any AST is
#   built.
#
#   The other three went the other way since this file was
#   written: KILL reached the engine, and answers MySQL's
#   ER_NO_SUCH_THREAD (1094, HY000) for an id nobody is
#   running, which is the answer MySQL gives too. A
#   sentinel that pins a parse error is a snapshot of a
#   parser; one that pins 1094 says something about the
#   server, which is the better thing to keep.
#
#   Recovery paths, for the four that remain:
#
#   - BACKUP DATABASE / RESTORE FROM → zeta uses
#     LSM-segment snapshots and log-replay for backup/
#     restore, not SQL-level commands. See the zeta
#     STORAGE_ENGINE_ANALYSIS.md for snapshot semantics.
#   - PURGE BINARY LOGS → zeta uses log-store
#     segments, not MySQL binlogs; rotation is internal
#     (no SQL handle).
#
# Adaptation notes:
#   # zeta-adapt: BACKUP DATABASE / RESTORE FROM / PURGE
#     BINARY LOGS error 1064 (shape d). Backup/restore
#     are out-of-band ops in zeta; binlog purge is
#     internal.

--echo === KILL of a thread that is not there → ER_NO_SUCH_THREAD (1094), three spellings ===

--error 1094
KILL 123;
--error 1094
KILL QUERY 456;
--error 1094
KILL CONNECTION 789;

--echo === BACKUP / RESTORE / PURGE — shape (d) "Expected: an SQL statement" ===

--error 1064
BACKUP DATABASE test TO 'foo';
--error 1064
RESTORE FROM 'foo';
--error 1064
PURGE BINARY LOGS BEFORE '2026-01-01';
--error 1064
PURGE BINARY LOGS TO 'log';
